{"content_hash": "c0187dda697d2c71", "version_id": 524, "article_id": 53, "level_code": "en_b2", "lang": "en", "level": 4, "level_label": "B2 中高级", "title": "Brown: UK homes would gain £5,000 from innovation", "title_zh": "布朗：靠创新，英国家庭能多赚五千镑", "has_translation": true, "topic": "science", "source": "guardian_science", "source_url": "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2026/oct/09/gordon-brown-british-households-5000-richer-innovation", "image": {"type": "photo", "url": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1751577474961-05c71804ed91?ixid=M3wxMDk3Mzk1fDB8MXxzZWFyY2h8Nnx8YnJpdGlzaCUyMGhvdXNlaG9sZHMlMjByaWNoZXJ8ZW58MHwwfHx8MTc5MTY3ODAxMXww&ixlib=rb-4.1.0&w=1600&q=75&fm=jpg&fit=max", "provider": "unsplash", "credit": "Photo by E R on Unsplash", "author": "E R", "credit_url": "https://unsplash.com/@100picrats?utm_source=daily-english-news&utm_medium=referral", "source": "Unsplash", "source_url": "https://unsplash.com/photos/the-union-jack-flies-outside-a-building-9mO8RH27w9A?utm_source=daily-english-news&utm_medium=referral", "fallback": {"type": "gradient", "from": "hsl(343, 42%, 62%)", "to": "hsl(21, 46%, 48%)", "label": "SCIENCE", "seed": "4bdfdd7dd72e"}}, "published_date": "2026-10-09", "word_count": 240, "reading_minutes": 1.2, "lead": "Gordon Brown says Britain could be far richer if it turned its best ideas into global companies. He calls on the government to put innovation at the centre of its plans.", "lead_zh": "戈登·布朗表示，如果英国能把最好的创意变成全球性企业，国家会富裕得多。他呼吁政府把创新放在各项计划的核心位置。", "preview": "The former prime minister, who led the UK from 2007 to 2010, argues that the next ten years will bring the greatest scientific breakthroughs", "preview_zh": "这位曾在2007年至2010年领导英国的 former 首相认为，未来十年将出现一个世纪以来最重大的科学突破。人工智能、量子计算和生物学的进步，可能有助于对抗癌症和痴呆症，并应对日益严重的抗生素耐药性问题。", "has_audio": true, "audio": {"id": 512, "url": "https://pub-aba43a6fb1db4dc08fede1dbc81f3241.r2.dev/audio/53/en_b2.mp3", "duration": 96.888, "size_bytes": 585072, "engine": "edge-tts", "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural", "word_count": 279, "has_timeline": true}, "detail_url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@fc48a26/content/data/versions/524.json", "paragraphs": ["The former prime minister, who led the UK from 2007 to 2010, argues that the next ten years will bring the greatest scientific breakthroughs in a century. Advances in AI, quantum computing and biology could help fight cancer and dementia, and tackle the growing resistance to antibiotics.", "Britain has strong assets, Brown writes. It has four of the world's top 10 universities, and with under 1% of the world's population it produces 6% of research papers and 12% of the most cited research. Yet only 36% of the public believes the country leads the world in science.", "The main problem is scale. Britain is good at startups, but slow to turn them into global success stories. The US has twice as many $1bn companies per person, and three-quarters of venture capital comes from overseas. Brown says innovation must sit at the heart of government policy and its 10-year plan.", "If Britain matched Swedish or Japanese levels of innovation, every household would be about £5,000 better off over time, he calculates, and national income would rise by £150bn a year. He also points to 127 innovative clusters across the UK that need long-term support, skills and devolved power.", "Brown ends with John F Kennedy's moon speech, saying this generation of Britons must not be left behind as AI speeds up. The challenge, he says, is not only to invent the future but to invest in it and own it in Britain."], "paragraphs_zh": ["这位曾在2007年至2010年领导英国的 former 首相认为，未来十年将出现一个世纪以来最重大的科学突破。人工智能、量子计算和生物学的进步，可能有助于对抗癌症和痴呆症，并应对日益严重的抗生素耐药性问题。", "布朗写道，英国拥有雄厚的资产。全球排名前十的大学中，英国占四所；英国人口不到全球的1%，却产出了6%的研究论文和12%的被引用最多的研究。然而，只有36%的公众认为英国在科学上处于世界领先地位。", "主要问题在于规模。英国擅长创办初创企业，却很难把它们做成全球性的成功故事。按人口计算，美国十亿美元级公司的数量是英国的两倍，而且四分之三的风险投资来自海外。布朗表示，创新必须成为政府政策及其十年计划的核心。", "他估算，如果英国的创新强度达到瑞典或日本的水平，长期来看每个家庭大约能多出5000英镑，国民收入每年将增加1500亿英镑。他还指出，全英有127个创新集群，它们需要长期支持、技能培训和下放的权力。", "布朗以肯尼迪的登月演讲作结，称在人工智能加速发展的时代，这一代英国人不能被落下。他说，挑战不仅在于发明未来，还在于投资未来，并让未来属于英国。"], "body": "The former prime minister, who led the UK from 2007 to 2010, argues that the next ten years will bring the greatest scientific breakthroughs in a century. Advances in AI, quantum computing and biology could help fight cancer and dementia, and tackle the growing resistance to antibiotics.\n\nBritain has strong assets, Brown writes. It has four of the world's top 10 universities, and with under 1% of the world's population it produces 6% of research papers and 12% of the most cited research. Yet only 36% of the public believes the country leads the world in science.\n\nThe main problem is scale. Britain is good at startups, but slow to turn them into global success stories. The US has twice as many $1bn companies per person, and three-quarters of venture capital comes from overseas. Brown says innovation must sit at the heart of government policy and its 10-year plan.\n\nIf Britain matched Swedish or Japanese levels of innovation, every household would be about £5,000 better off over time, he calculates, and national income would rise by £150bn a year. He also points to 127 innovative clusters across the UK that need long-term support, skills and devolved power.\n\nBrown ends with John F Kennedy's moon speech, saying this generation of Britons must not be left behind as AI speeds up. The challenge, he says, is not only to invent the future but to invest in it and own it in Britain.", "body_zh": "这位曾在2007年至2010年领导英国的 former 首相认为，未来十年将出现一个世纪以来最重大的科学突破。人工智能、量子计算和生物学的进步，可能有助于对抗癌症和痴呆症，并应对日益严重的抗生素耐药性问题。\n\n布朗写道，英国拥有雄厚的资产。全球排名前十的大学中，英国占四所；英国人口不到全球的1%，却产出了6%的研究论文和12%的被引用最多的研究。然而，只有36%的公众认为英国在科学上处于世界领先地位。\n\n主要问题在于规模。英国擅长创办初创企业，却很难把它们做成全球性的成功故事。按人口计算，美国十亿美元级公司的数量是英国的两倍，而且四分之三的风险投资来自海外。布朗表示，创新必须成为政府政策及其十年计划的核心。\n\n他估算，如果英国的创新强度达到瑞典或日本的水平，长期来看每个家庭大约能多出5000英镑，国民收入每年将增加1500亿英镑。他还指出，全英有127个创新集群，它们需要长期支持、技能培训和下放的权力。\n\n布朗以肯尼迪的登月演讲作结，称在人工智能加速发展的时代，这一代英国人不能被落下。他说，挑战不仅在于发明未来，还在于投资未来，并让未来属于英国。", "text": "The former prime minister, who led the UK from 2007 to 2010, argues that the next ten years will bring the greatest scientific breakthroughs in a century. Advances in AI, quantum computing and biology could help fight cancer and dementia, and tackle the growing resistance to antibiotics. Britain has strong assets, Brown writes. It has four of the world's top 10 universities, and with under 1% of the world's population it produces 6% of research papers and 12% of the most cited research. Yet only 36% of the public believes the country leads the world in science. The main problem is scale. Britain is good at startups, but slow to turn them into global success stories. The US has twice as many $1bn companies per person, and three-quarters of venture capital comes from overseas. Brown says innovation must sit at the heart of government policy and its 10-year plan. If Britain matched Swedish or Japanese levels of innovation, every household would be about £5,000 better off over time, he calculates, and national income would rise by £150bn a year. He also points to 127 innovative clusters across the UK that need long-term support, skills and devolved power. Brown ends with John F Kennedy's moon speech, saying this generation of Britons must not be left behind as AI speeds up. The challenge, he says, is not only to invent the future but to invest in it and own it in Britain.", "vocab": [{"word": "breakthrough", "pos": "noun", "zh": "突破", "note": "常与 make 搭配：make a breakthrough", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "resistance", "pos": "noun", "zh": "耐药性；抵抗", "note": "医学语境常指抗生素耐药性", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "asset", "pos": "noun", "zh": "资产；优势", "note": "可指人或资源等宝贵的东西", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "startup", "pos": "noun", "zh": "初创企业", "note": "新兴公司，尤指科技类", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "scale-up", "pos": "noun", "zh": "规模化企业", "note": "由初创发展壮大的公司", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "venture capital", "pos": "phrase", "zh": "风险投资", "note": "投入高风险新创企业的资金", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "innovation", "pos": "noun", "zh": "创新", "note": "形容词为 innovative", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "household", "pos": "noun", "zh": "家庭", "note": "指一户人家，常用于经济报道", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "devolve", "pos": "verb", "zh": "下放（权力）", "note": "常与 power 搭配使用", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "cluster", "pos": "noun", "zh": "集群；聚集区", "note": "此处指创新企业集中的地区", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "vanguard", "pos": "noun", "zh": "先锋；前列", "note": "in the vanguard 意为处于领先", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "tackle", "pos": "verb", "zh": "应对；解决", "note": "常与 problem、issue 搭配", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}], "title_original": "British households would be £5,000 richer if we were more innovative. Let’s unleash our talents | Gordon Brown", "summary_original": "We have a deep reservoir of assets, but struggle to turn small companies into global success stories. Innovation needs to be at the heart of government policy\nGordon Brown was UK prime minister from 2007 to 2010\nThe coming 10 years are almost certain to be the decade that sees the greatest scientific breakthroughs in a century. The question is whether advances now under way in AI, quantum computing and biology can address cancer, find ways to treat or prevent dementia, and overcome the\ngrowing resistance\nto antibiotics. Can we find sustainable ways to address our energy needs and protect the environment at the same time? Can AI transform the way we deliver education, health and social care to the benefit of millions, as well as advance modern manufacturing strength? And what role can Britain’s inventors, investors and industrialists play in developing these transformative technologies?\nWhile the US and China are the world’s obvious innovation powerhouses, the UK also has enormous assets, as the Innovation Nation Conference in Manchester addressed by the prime minister, Andy Burnham, will hear today. We are home to\nfour of the world’s\ntop 10 universities and 16 of the top 100, five ", "body_original": "British households would be £5,000 richer if we were more innovative. Let’s unleash our talents\nGordon Brown\nWe have a deep reservoir of assets, but struggle to turn small companies into global success stories. Innovation needs to be at the heart of government policy\nGordon Brown was UK prime minister from 2007 to 2010\nT\nhe coming 10 years are almost certain to be the decade that sees the greatest scientific breakthroughs in a century. The question is whether advances now under way in AI, quantum computing and biology can address cancer, find ways to treat or prevent dementia, and overcome the\ngrowing resistance\nto antibiotics. Can we find sustainable ways to address our energy needs and protect the environment at the same time? Can AI transform the way we deliver education, health and social care to the benefit of millions, as well as advance modern manufacturing strength? And what role can Britain’s inventors, investors and industrialists play in developing these transformative technologies?\nWhile the US and China are the world’s obvious innovation powerhouses, the UK also has enormous assets, as the Innovation Nation Conference in Manchester addressed by the prime minister, Andy Burnham, will hear today. We are home to\nfour of the world’s\ntop 10 universities and 16 of the top 100, five of which are in Europe’s top 10 for spin-outs, startups based on intellectual property produced through a university’s research. With less than 1% of the world’s population, the\nUK produces\n6% of the world’s research papers, nearly 9% of citations and 12% of the most highly cited research.\nOur research expertise is one of the many reasons we have been at the forefront of recent inventions:\nfrom AlphaFold\n, which predicts how proteins behave, to viral-vector vaccine technology and AI that detects cancer as reliably as radiologists. Britain already sits at the intersection of AI, quantum computing and synthetic biology. The question then is: why, having led the first Industrial Revolution, should the UK not be the world’s great innovation nation once again?\nWe know the public have an entrepreneurial spirit, and back this vision. New polling for this week’s conference finds that\n75% of people\nagree that innovation is essential for creating UK economic growth.\nBut it is disappointing that\nonly 36%\nof the British public\nbelieves the country is a world leader in science. It is time for innovation to move to the forefront of government policy and the\n10-year plan\nthe government is preparing. If we were to raise Britain to Swedish or Japanese levels of innovation intensity we would, over time, leave every household £5,000 better off (according to my calculations), and increase our national income by £150bn a year as higher investment in research and development feeds through into productivity. And a jobs plan on the scale that\nOur Scottish Future\nhas proposed for Scotland would mean about 3m new jobs across the UK.\nWe can identify the major hurdles to overcome. While the UK is good at startups and spin-outs, we are slow to turn startups into scale-ups, and even slower to turn scale-ups into global success stories. Per head of population, the US has double the number of $1bn companies and nearly three times as many $10bn-plus companies than the UK. And even when we do scale up at home, three-quarters of venture capital investment comes from overseas.\nTurning this around will require a government to keep tackling the bottlenecks that are inevitably created from innovation. Speakers from future-facing companies at today’s conference will set out the steps that we need to consider. These will range from making sure firms can access the money they need to grow from UK investors, to new incentives for investors, to public-private partnerships – as happens in Germany, France and Italy – capitalised by the\nBritish Business Bank\nand the\nnational wealth fund\n. Competition policy must favour new entrants against vested interests and be aligned with a favourable tax regime, such as through research and development credits that favour companies of all sizes.\nResearch\nfor the conference\nhas identified 127 innovative clusters covering every region and nation of the UK, many of which will succeed if we are willing to give long-term support to the infrastructure, skills training and research and development support from UK Research and Innovation\nthey need to grow. In order to harness this potential, more power needs to be devolved to enable mayors and regional authorities to act locally while ensuring access to global markets.\nBritain does not lack genius or great assets. Every nation, including ours, must do more to find its “\nlost Einsteins\n” – the young people in lower-income areas who have the talent but not yet the opportunity to show their creative genius. What we need now is to create a chain of hope that connects all people to intellectual discovery and deployment, to startups and scale-ups, and to global leadership – all aligned around a shared national purpose.\nSixty-four years ago, John F Kennedy declared that the US would lead a nationwide effort to be first on the moon. But the space initiative was not his first choice. He had wanted a national project that everybody could feel part of, and revealed he would have preferred to have chosen a project on Earth – if he could have found one with the same power to inspire a national mission that the whole population could enthusiastically embrace.\nToday, we can choose to be the innovation nation. “This generation does not intend to founder in the backwash of the coming age,” said Kennedy of the US. And this generation of Britons has no intention of being left behind as the age of AI\naccelerates. Today’s event, spearheaded by the prime minister in Manchester – itself a city of innovation – will send a message that we mean to be in the global vanguard; not because, as Kennedy said, it is easy to do, but even though it is hard. For the challenge now is to do more than invent the future. We must invest in it, manufacture it and, wherever possible, own it here in Britain.\nGordon Brown was UK prime minister from 2007 to 2010\nExplore more on these topics\nScience\nOpinion\nTechnology sector\nTechnology policy\nUniversities\nAI (artificial intelligence)\nMedical research\ncomment\nShare\nReuse this content", "fetched_at": "2026-10-11T00:20:09.769248", "timeline": [{"i": 0, "w": "The", "s": 0.1, "e": 0.275, "sm": 100, "em": 275, "cs": 0, "ce": 3, "si": 0}, {"i": 1, "w": "former", "s": 0.287, "e": 0.65, "sm": 287, "em": 649, "cs": 4, "ce": 10, "si": 0}, {"i": 2, "w": "prime", "s": 0.662, "e": 0.925, "sm": 662, "em": 925, "cs": 11, "ce": 16, "si": 0}, {"i": 3, "w": "minister", "s": 0.938, "e": 1.462, "sm": 937, "em": 1462, "cs": 17, "ce": 25, "si": 0}, {"i": 4, "w": ",", "s": 1.462, "e": 1.775, "sm": 1462, "em": 1775, "cs": 25, "ce": 26, "si": 0}, {"i": 5, "w": "who", "s": 1.775, "e": 1.875, "sm": 1775, "em": 1875, "cs": 27, "ce": 30, "si": 0}, {"i": 6, "w": "led", "s": 1.887, "e": 2.112, "sm": 1887, "em": 2112, "cs": 31, "ce": 34, "si": 0}, {"i": 7, "w": "the", "s": 2.125, "e": 2.175, "sm": 2125, "em": 2175, "cs": 35, "ce": 38, "si": 0}, {"i": 8, "w": "UK", "s": 2.188, "e": 2.663, "sm": 2187, "em": 2662, "cs": 39, "ce": 41, "si": 0}, {"i": 9, "w": "from", "s": 2.675, "e": 3.293, "sm": 2675, "em": 3292, "cs": 42, "ce": 46, "si": 0}, {"i": 10, "w": "2007", "s": 3.293, "e": 3.911, "sm": 3292, "em": 3910, "cs": 47, "ce": 51, "si": 0}, {"i": 11, "w": "to", "s": 3.911, "e": 4.22, "sm": 3910, "em": 4219, "cs": 52, "ce": 54, "si": 0}, {"i": 12, "w": "2010", "s": 4.22, "e": 4.838, "sm": 4219, "em": 4837, "cs": 55, "ce": 59, "si": 0}, {"i": 13, "w": ",", "s": 4.838, "e": 5.225, "sm": 4837, "em": 5225, "cs": 59, "ce": 60, "si": 0}, {"i": 14, "w": "argues", "s": 5.225, "e": 5.688, "sm": 5225, "em": 5687, "cs": 61, "ce": 67, "si": 0}, {"i": 15, "w": "that", "s": 5.7